{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Six days you shall eat unleavened bread.</b> But did He not already mention above, ״seven days you shall eat unleavened bread upon it״? Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: why was the seventh singled out from the general category? To teach about the entire category from which it was singled out — for this is one of the thirteen hermeneutical principles by which the Torah is interpreted — to say: just as the seventh is optional, so too the six days are optional: if he wishes, he eats unleavened bread or fruits; unlike the first night, concerning which it is stated (Exodus 12:18): ״בערב תאכלו מצות״ — the verse fixed it as an obligation." "<b>And on the seventh day, a closing assembly (atzeret).</b> It is called by the term עצרת because Israel is held back and gathered on the festivals to synagogues and houses of study, and its Targum is כנישין. And you should be roused to understand that the seventh day of Passover is the sefirah of Yesod, and Scripture called it עצרת just as the eighth day of Sukkot is called, which is the eighth day adjacent to it, and this was already explained at the end of parashat Pinchas (Numbers 29:35) in the verse ״ביום השמיני עצרת״." "<b>You shall do no work.</b> Concerning the commandment of Sabbath it is stated (Exodus 20:10): ״לא תעשה כל מלאכה״ — you shall do no manner of work; but concerning the Festival He said merely ״מלאכה״ — work. And one cannot say of it ״כל מלאכה״ (all manner of work), since food preparation (אוכל נפש) is permitted."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:43.675598807Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}